renderer: a function from one file to nodes. The general idea behind shelf,
page and diff; python's page is the case that made it plain.

  file --renderer--> nodes --> engine
                     details#id > summary(heading, edge...) + body

  The node is the contract (medium/page.html, checked by medium/page/run.mjs).
  The engine is keys, folds, find, address. It does not know the kind. A
  renderer fills heading, edge and body and nothing else.

Where a renderer runs is a detail, not a design:

  kind        renderer            runs        why there
  .md  .js    shelf outline       the page    text only; sections() / defs()
  .diff       shelf outline       the page    text only
  .py         page/py             terminal    needs ast; the page cannot
  diff.md     page/diff           terminal    needs ast and two trees
  a new kind  a new renderer      either      text -> page; parse -> terminal

Rule: a kind that needs a parser gets a generator and its own page; a kind that
is only text stays in the shelf's outline. The kind picks the renderer by file
extension, so a new kind never changes the shelf or the engine.

Two views of every file: rendered and source
  The renderer's page is the rendered view; the file as it is on disk is the
  source view. A key flips between them on the same place, and the node's id
  is what keeps the place (rendered Field.broken_constraint <-> its line range
  in the source, 206..225).

    rendered   renderer(file) -> nodes        what it means, folded
    source     the file, line numbers        what it says, whole
       flip    same id, same cursor

  Source is never lost: the rendered body already holds the code as written
  (todo/studio/refactor, "body"), so rendered is a view over source, not a copy
  of it. A kind with no renderer shows source only. That is what the shelf does
  today for any file it does not know.
  Open: where the flip key lives (the shelf's keys, page.html's engine, or
  both); and whether a source view needs ids at all, or only line ranges.

What it buys
- one checker for all: node medium/page/run.mjs on any page a renderer made
- a better renderer replaces a worse one without touching the rest (the
  shelf's plain python rows, then page/py's ast; the node does not change)
- the same renderer feeds a page, a terminal pane or an app: the app case in
  todo/studio/studio changes who runs it, not what it makes

Open
- stale: a made page goes out of date when its file changes. Who runs the
  renderer is v diff's question too: you now, a watcher later
- the shelf opening a .py: link to the made page, or load its nodes in place.
  Start with the link; embed if the keys are missed
- python is drawn twice now that page/py exists: the shelf's plain rows (step
  3's def-line heading) and the made page. The rows are the quick outline; they
  grow no rail

Where it came from: todo/studio/refactor (the outline's steps), page/diff, and
the call that the rail needs the ast: a lexer in the browser could only
approximate it.
